Diameter of Cylinder minus radius divided data I've been working on this problem, but I don't know how to do it (I'm studying for the math GRE subject test). Here goes:
An open cylindrical barrel has diameter $D$ inches and height $H > D$ inches.
A diameter line is painted on the outside surface.  If the barrel is placed
on its side and rolled, so that at all times the painted diameter line
is on the bottom of the barrel, the paint will again touch each
plane of the barrel's surface exactly $k$ times for some integer $k$.
What is $k$? Find for what radius diameter ratio:
a) the number of planes $\tau$ in contact will be $1$ each time the
cylinder is rolled once;
b) the number of planes $\tau$ in contact will be $2$ each time the
cylinder is rolled once.

The general reason that there won't be more than two planes at resting is readily apparent.

Now the danger is that the painted line on top won't be level with the one below the supporting axle. When theointed surface of this slope is level with the supporting axle, we have $D=H$, and that's when we wrap the rolling wheel $n$ times:

For this to happen, say at angle $\alpha,$ so $n\alpha=180^\circ\!\!,$ we also have:

*

*$\Theta = H\alpha = D\alpha$, for rolling on a flat surface subject to gravity;

*$\cos\alpha = (\frac{H-D}{D}),$ for rolling on a slope.

We combine to get: $n=(D^2 [-(D-H)^8+H^4]^{\frac 12})/\pi,$ which is smallest at its extreme values, i.e.: $D:H = 1:\sqrt 2$ vs. $D:H = \sqrt 2:1,$ where $D=H,$ these are also the only possibilities.

Edit in original answer: Since $n$ is irrational in this case..., I don't know an exact answer, but in must-old Putnam we could prove: The number of $\phi$-lengths (\phi-tangent, right?) intercepting $\pi r^2$ is: $\frac {\pi r^2}{\phi r}=360^\circ / \phi$

